# Line-ending policy. This repo is authored on Windows, where Git's installer
# sets core.autocrlf=true system-wide: blobs are stored LF, but every text file
# is smudged to CRLF on checkout. That is invisible until it isn't — see below.

# Everything text-like is stored LF in the repository.
* text=auto

# Shell scripts must be LF in the WORKING TREE too, not just in the blob.
# A CRLF shebang makes the kernel look for an interpreter named "/bin/bash\r",
# so `./qc_check.sh` dies with `bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M`. Running it as
# `bash scripts/qc_check.sh` masks the problem; direct execution does not.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Docs and structured data: LF in the working tree as well. Without this,
# `git diff` prints "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it"
# for every one of these files — pure noise that hides real warnings.
*.md   text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Git's own metadata files, so this file does not warn about itself.
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf

# Subtitles are deliberately NOT pinned to LF. They are tracked run evidence
# (see .gitignore), SubRip conventionally uses CRLF, and `* text=auto` above
# already stores them LF in the repo. Leave the working-tree form alone.

*.png binary
